WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:24.040
<v Ira Glass>I was a reporter for years in the Chicago Public schools, covering all the different ways that people tried to fix and improve those schools. And one of the big debates-- not just among the parents and politicians, but inside the schools as well-- was how much could we expect teachers to actually accomplish? Like, how much could they do, really, with the students they were given?
<v Ira Glass>我曾在芝加哥公立学校当了多年记者，报道过人们尝试修复和改善这些学校的各种方法。其中一个重大的辩论——不仅是在家长和政客之间，甚至在学校内部——就是我们到底能期望老师取得多大的成就？也就是说，面对分给他们的学生，他们真的能做到什么程度？

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:35.700
<v Ira Glass>Some teachers definitely were the heroic we-can-do-better types. We need to try everything. They were trying all kinds of reforms and fixes to make their schools improve. Others shrugged.
<v Ira Glass>有些老师绝对是那种充满英雄主义的“我们可以做得更好”的类型。我们需要尝试一切。他们尝试了各种各样的改革和补救措施来改善学校。其他人则耸耸肩。

00:00:35.700 --> 00:01:03.830
<v Ira Glass>This population of kids, they would tell you, arrives with such deficits-- no books at home, violent neighborhoods, the kind of family problems that would distract anybody from school. These teachers would say, we're doing as well with them as anybody could. You know, if dozens of kids show up in your classroom each year with terrible reading ability and in need of remedial writing instruction, how exactly are you supposed to bring them up to grade level on the state standardized tests in the one year you have with them?
<v Ira Glass>他们会告诉你，这群孩子入学时就带着各种缺陷——家里没有书、身处暴力社区、家庭问题足以让任何人无法专注于学业。这些老师会说，我们能教到这种程度已经是尽力了。你知道，如果每年有几十个孩子带着糟糕的阅读能力进入你的课堂，而且需要补习写作指导，在这一年的时间里，你究竟该如何让他们达到州标准化考试的年级水平呢？

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:31.150
<v Ira Glass>One of the things that's been interesting about the teacher's strike in the Chicago schools is that it's actually gotten people talking about some very basic things in education, and things like these. Like for instance, one of the big issues is standardized testing. How much should teachers be evaluated on the test scores of their students? Where you stand on that one pretty much comes down to whether you think that teachers have a lot of power to raise those scores or not.
<v Ira Glass>关于芝加哥学校教师罢工，有趣的一点是，它确实让人们开始讨论教育中一些非常基础的问题。例如，其中一个大问题是标准化考试。教师应该在多大程度上根据学生的考试成绩来接受评估？你在这个问题上的立场很大程度上取决于你认为老师在提高这些分数方面是否有很大的影响力。

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:55.630
<v Ira Glass>There's an essay that's being published this Sunday in the New York Times by Alex Kotlowitz, a reporter who writes a lot about kids and poverty in Chicago. And he points out how much we expect from teachers, especially teachers in a school system like Chicago's where 87% of the students come from low income families. He writes that he's been reporting in a high school lately where the staff has transformed and improved the school in lots of ways.
<v Ira Glass>本周日《纽约时报》将刊登亚历克斯·科特洛维茨（Alex Kotlowitz）的一篇文章，他是一位经常报道芝加哥儿童和贫困问题的记者。他指出，我们对老师的期望太高了，特别是在芝加哥这样的学校系统里，87%的学生来自低收入家庭。他写道，他最近在报道一所高中，那里的教职员工在很多方面对学校进行了改造和改善。

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:15.930
<v Ira Glass>"And yet each day I spend there," he writes, "I witness one heartbreaking scene after another. A young girl who yells at one of the school's social workers, 'This is no way to live,' and then breaks down in tears. Because of problems at home, she's had to move in with a friend's family, and there's not enough food to go around. A young man has retreated into his shell having witnessed a murder over the summer.
<v Ira Glass>“然而，我每天在那里度过，”他写道，“我目睹了一个又一个令人心碎的场景。一个小女孩对着学校的一名社会工作者喊道：‘这根本不是人过的日子，’然后泪流满面。因为家庭问题，她不得不搬到朋友家住，家里食物都不够吃。一个年轻人因为在夏天目睹了一起谋杀案，从此变得封闭自己。”

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:36.290
<v Ira Glass>The stories are all too familiar. And yet somehow, we've come to believe that with really good teachers and longer school days and rigorous testing, we can transform their lives. We've imagined teachers as lazy, excuse-making quasi-professionals or, alternately, as lifesavers. But the truth, of course, is more complicated."
<v Ira Glass>这些故事太熟悉了。然而不知何故，我们开始相信，只要有真正优秀的老师、更长的在校时间和严格的考试，我们就能改变他们的生活。我们将老师想象成懒惰、找借口、半吊子的专业人士，或者反过来，将他们视为救世主。但事实当然要复杂得多。

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:55.820
<v Ira Glass>I bring all this up here today because there is a new way to look at all this, a new body of science that's just starting to get discussed in a widespread way. It looks at what a difficult home life can do to the brain of a school kid-- literally to the biology of the brain-- and how it makes it difficult to learn.
<v Ira Glass>我今天之所以提起这些，是因为有一种看待这一切的新视角，一套新的科学理论刚刚开始被广泛讨论。它着眼于艰难的家庭生活会对学龄儿童的大脑产生什么影响——甚至是字面意义上的大脑生物学影响——以及它是如何导致学习困难的。

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:05.280
<v Ira Glass>And this science suggests all kinds of things that schools could be doing that could improve teaching, get through to huge numbers of kids that teachers have a hard time getting through to today.
<v Ira Glass>而这门科学表明，学校可以做各种各样的事情来改善教学，接触到那些老师目前难以触及的广大孩子。

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:31.610
<v Ira Glass>As school starts up again, we thought here at the radio show that it would be nice to turn away from the political questions and the union questions and the money questions and all the regular stuff that people argue about when they argue about schools all the time and turn instead today for the beginning of school to something more optimistic and forward-looking, something that feels big and Earth-shaking and, I have to say, kind of exciting. That is what we're going to be talking about today.
<v Ira Glass>随着学校再次开学，我们在广播节目中认为，与其讨论政治问题、工会问题、资金问题以及人们在谈论学校时总是争论的所有常规话题，不如在开学之际谈谈一些更乐观、更有前瞻性的内容，一些感觉宏大、震撼人心，而且我不得不说，令人兴奋的事情。这就是我们今天要谈论的内容。

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:45.990
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》（This American Life），由国际公共广播电台（PRI）发行。请继续收听。

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:59.750
<v Ira Glass>Act One-- No, These Things Will Not Be on the Final Exam. OK, so let's start with the most basic question, a question that is so big, it is almost embarrassing to ask out loud. What should kids be learning at school?
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——不，这些内容不会出现在期末考试中。好吧，让我们从最基本的问题开始，这个问题太大了，说出来甚至有点尴尬。孩子们在学校应该学什么？

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:20.150
<v Ira Glass>You know, people argue about this. Schools are established pushing this approach or that one about the best way to teach. And I learned about this shift in the way that people are answering this question, this whole new set of theories and ideas that are in the air right now from this new book, this book that came out this month that is trying to document that shift and explain what these ideas are about.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，人们对此争论不休。学校建立起来是为了推行这种或那种教学方法。我从一本这个月刚出版的新书里了解到了人们回答这个问题的方式正在发生转变，这是一套全新的理论和观点，旨在记录这种转变并解释这些想法是什么。

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:28.800
<v Ira Glass>Some of these ideas have been around for years but are now backed up by new data and tied to research about what goes on in kids' brains. Some are new, based on new research.
<v Ira Glass>其中一些想法已经存在多年，但现在有了新的数据支持，并与关于儿童大脑运作的研究联系在一起。有些则是基于新研究的新观点。

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:40.990
<v Paul Tough>There are educators involved in this, psychologists, economists, neuroscientists. They're not all working together. But they're starting to make connections. And I feel like they're coming to a similar conclusion.
<v Paul Tough>参与其中的有教育工作者、心理学家、经济学家、神经科学家。他们并非都在一起工作，但他们开始建立联系，而且我觉得他们正在得出相似的结论。

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:49.990
<v Ira Glass>This is Paul Tough. He's a reporter who writes about new approaches to educating children. He's been on our program now and then in the past. And he's the one who wrote this new book.
<v Ira Glass>这是保罗·塔夫（Paul Tough）。他是一位报道教育儿童新方法的记者。他过去曾偶尔出现在我们的节目中，也是这本书的作者。

00:04:49.990 --> 00:05:04.950
<v Ira Glass>He says that to understand this shift in thinking, you have to first understand where things were before the shift. Up until now, what's been emphasised in most schools is something called-- and jargon word here, but there's no way around this-- something they call cognitive development.
<v Ira Glass>他说，要理解这种思维的转变，首先必须了解转变之前的情况。到目前为止，大多数学校强调的是一种被称为——这里用个术语，但无法避免——所谓“认知发展”的东西。

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:18.910
<v Ira Glass>And what "cognitive development" means is smarts the way that you think about smarts in school-- the stuff that gets measured on IQ tests and standardized math and reading achievement tests that kids take most places. And it's not like that stuff isn't important.
<v Ira Glass>而“认知发展”的意思就是聪明才智，也就是你在学校里认为的聪明才智——即智商测试以及孩子们在大多数地方参加的标准化数学和阅读成绩测试中所衡量的东西。并不是说这些东西不重要。

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:35.680
<v Paul Tough>Well, I'm definitely not saying that none of that stuff matters at all. I'm saying that we have put way too much emphasis on it, and especially in the early years. I think it's especially parents of very young kids who get really anxious that their kids need all of these skills and all of this information really early on. I think--
<v Paul Tough>嗯，我绝对不是说这些东西完全不重要。我是说我们对它的强调太过分了，特别是在早年阶段。我认为特别是那些非常年幼孩子的家长，他们非常焦虑，觉得孩子需要很早就掌握所有这些技能和信息。我想——

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.380
<v Ira Glass>There's a whole industry that came out of that.
<v Ira Glass>由此产生了一个完整的产业链。

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:58.600
<v Paul Tough>Exactly. I think it's part of these preschool classes, Junior Kumon, all of these flash cards and brain building toys and the right kind of videos and DVDs from early on to try to boost their cognitive skills at an earlier and earlier age.
<v Paul Tough>没错。我认为这是学前班、幼儿公文式学习法（Junior Kumon）、所有这些闪示卡、健脑玩具以及从早期就开始提供的各种正确类型的视频和DVD的一部分，试图在越来越小的年龄提升他们的认知技能。

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.840
<v Ira Glass>So that's the old way of looking at this. What's our new understanding?
<v Ira Glass>所以那是看待这个问题的旧方式。我们新的理解是什么？

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:17.020
<v Paul Tough>Where I think they're heading is this idea that what matters most in a child's success is not just cognitive skill. It's not just reading and math and IQ. It is this very different set of qualities.
<v Paul Tough>我认为他们的方向是这种想法：孩子成功最重要的不仅是认知技能。不仅仅是阅读、数学和智商。而是一套完全不同的品质。

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:23.140
<v Ira Glass>And you write that the guy who's kind of at the heart of this way of thinking is an economist named Heckman, right?
<v Ira Glass>你写道，这种思维方式的核心人物是一位名叫赫克曼（Heckman）的经济学家，对吧？

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:44.360
<v Paul Tough>Yeah, James Heckman. He's a Nobel Prize winning economist at the University of Chicago. If there's any one figure who is trying to pull together all of this research, it's him. He puts together these conferences where he invites psychologists and neuroscientists and economists, people who don't usually have much connection, to get together and talk about some of these ideas and some of these issues.
<v Paul Tough>是的，詹姆斯·赫克曼（James Heckman）。他是芝加哥大学的诺贝尔经济学奖得主。如果说有哪个人试图将所有这些研究汇集在一起，那就是他。他组织了一些会议，邀请心理学家、神经科学家和经济学家，这些平时没什么交集的人聚在一起，讨论其中一些想法和议题。

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:51.070
<v Paul Tough>The thing that really got Heckman started down this path was a study that he did about the GED.
<v Paul Tough>让赫克曼真正走上这条路的是他做的一项关于同等学力证书（GED）的研究。

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:59.530
<v Ira Glass>The GED, of course, is the high school equivalency exam. If you didn't graduate from high school, you can take the GED. And if you pass, it counts the same as if you graduated.
<v Ira Glass>GED当然是高中同等学力考试。如果你没有从高中毕业，你可以参加GED考试。如果你通过了，就等同于高中毕业。

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:05.320
<v James Heckman>Well, I'll give you-- it's a personal anecdote. This was in the early 1990s.
<v James Heckman>嗯，我给你讲个个人轶事。这是在20世纪90年代初。

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:15.710
<v Ira Glass>When I invited Heckman into the studio to chat, he told me how blown away he was when he first encountered the GED. It was the early 1990s, like he said. He was in Texas doing a study about something else.
<v Ira Glass>当我邀请赫克曼到演播室聊天时，他告诉我他第一次接触GED时是多么震惊。那是20世纪90年代初，正如他所说。他在德克萨斯州做另一项研究。

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:37.560
<v James Heckman>And they pointed to this classroom and said, "You realize that these people will be studying for their GEDs. And in a few weeks, maybe a few months, you can convert essentially high school dropouts, people who were even intermediate school dropouts, into high school graduates." And I said, "That's amazing."
<v James Heckman>他们指着这间教室说：“你意识到这些人将要学习GED了吗？几周，也许几个月后，你基本上就可以把高中辍学生，甚至是初中辍学生，变成高中毕业生。”我说：“这太不可思议了。”

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:44.150
<v James Heckman>So I'm an economist. And that really started to surprise me. And as a result--
<v James Heckman>所以我是个经济学家，这真的让我开始感到惊讶。结果——

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:49.300
<v Ira Glass>Wait, why was that surprising if what they were testing was just you've learned this information?
<v Ira Glass>等等，如果他们测试的只是你学到了这些信息，那为什么会令人惊讶呢？

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:58.150
<v James Heckman>Well, it was surprising, you know, in a few months, you could actually teach people the knowledge that they would acquire in four years of high school.
<v James Heckman>嗯，令人惊讶的是，你知道，在几个月内，你居然可以教给人们他们在四年高中里学到的知识。

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:58.610
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我明白了。

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:25.780
<v James Heckman>Maybe a couple more. So it seemed to me-- really, the average preparation time for a GED is like 32 hours. And the average amount of study time for a student in high school is around 1,000 hours per year. And you say, "Well, wait, that's like 3,000, 4,000 hours versus 32." And from an economic standpoint, if that were true, it would be a real miracle. And it would be very cost effective. All eighth graders should take the GEDs.
<v James Heckman>也许再多几个月。所以在我看来——实际上，GED的平均准备时间大约是32小时。而高中生平均学习时间每年大约是1000小时。你说，“好吧，等等，那是3000到4000小时对比32小时。”从经济学角度看，如果这是真的，那将是一个真正的奇迹，而且非常有成本效益。所有八年级学生都应该参加GED考试。

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:26.740
<v Ira Glass>Instead of going to high school?
<v Ira Glass>而不是去上高中？

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.800
<v James Heckman>Instead of going to high school.
<v James Heckman>而不是去上高中。

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.920
<v Ira Glass>We would save ourselves a lot of money.
<v Ira Glass>我们会节省很多钱。

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:39.840
<v James Heckman>Save ourselves a lot of money. Save ourselves a lot of time.
<v James Heckman>节省很多钱。节省很多时间。

00:08:39.840 --> 00:09:00.830
<v Ira Glass>So Heckman kept thinking about this. If it were true, should we even have high school? It raised some questions. Which led him to wonder, well, is it really true that the GED was equivalent to graduating from high school? Did the GED students really go on to do as well as the graduating high school students in life?
<v Ira Glass>所以赫克曼一直在思考这个问题。如果这是真的，我们甚至应该有高中吗？这引起了一些疑问。这让他开始怀疑，GED真的等同于高中毕业吗？GED学生在生活中真的能像高中毕业生那样表现出色吗？

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:07.590
<v Ira Glass>So he devised a study that looked at what happened over time to people with GEDs versus people who graduated from high school.
<v Ira Glass>于是他设计了一项研究，观察GED持有者与高中毕业生随着时间推移的情况。

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:31.530
<v James Heckman>And so when we follow these people into adult life, follow them many years, what we found was consistently GEDs are performing slightly better than dropouts who didn't go on and take the GED, people who really dropped out of high school, but nowhere near as well as high school graduates. And that was in terms of performance in earnings, performance in occupation.
<v James Heckman>所以当我们跟踪这些人进入成年生活，跟踪多年后，我们发现GED持有者的表现始终略好于那些没有去考GED的辍学生，也就是真正的高中辍学生，但远远不如高中毕业生。这体现在收入表现和职业表现上。

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:47.670
<v James Heckman>We find that they consistently fail, whether it's going on in college. The success rates in college are very low. The success rates in the military are very low. The success rates in marriage are very low. They get married, but they drop out of marriage. These people drop out of virtually everything they start.
<v James Heckman>我们发现他们始终表现不佳，无论是上大学。上大学的成功率很低。参军的成功率很低。婚姻的成功率很低。他们会结婚，但婚姻会破裂。这些人几乎退出了他们开始的所有事情。

00:09:47.670 --> 00:10:01.490
<v Ira Glass>Now, if you think about it, this shouldn't be so surprising. If you can't manage to follow the rules and do what's assigned and keep your ass in a chair for four years of high school, of course you might be somebody who has trouble applying yourself later in life. Of course, you might be different from a high school grad.
<v Ira Glass>现在，如果你想想，这不应该令人惊讶。如果你不能设法遵守规则，完成布置的任务，并且在高中坚持坐四年冷板凳，那么你当然可能是一个在以后的生活中难以努力的人。当然，你可能与高中毕业生有所不同。

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:25.620
<v Ira Glass>But what struck Heckman is that this didn't show up in the test results. Our entire education system is organized around the idea that testing and the kind of smarts that you can measure on a test, are the most important information we could have about a student. That's how we evaluate whether a school is well-run. There are kids  who do better on standardized tests. That's at the heart of huge policy initiatives, like No Child Left Behind.
<v Ira Glass>但让赫克曼感到震惊的是，这并没有在测试结果中显示出来。我们整个教育系统都是围绕着一个想法组织的：测试和你在测试中衡量的聪明才智是我们能拥有的关于学生的最重要的信息。这就是我们评估学校是否管理得当的方式。有些孩子在标准化测试中表现更好。这是重大政策举措的核心，比如《不让一个孩子掉队》（No Child Left Behind）。

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:41.080
<v Ira Glass>But here was a test, the GED, that said that millions of people were just as smart as high school graduates. If they passed the GED, it proved that their cognitive skills were just as good. But these people were failing, which led Heckman to conclude--
<v Ira Glass>但这里有一个测试，GED，它声称数百万人和高中毕业生一样聪明。如果他们通过了GED，证明他们的认知技能同样好。但这些人却在失败，这导致赫克曼得出结论——

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:57.760
<v James Heckman>That these test scores explain only a tiny fraction of the variability among individuals-- who's successful and who's not-- and that other factors are out there that aren't measured that aren't even accounted for in public policy that make a big difference. And so I said, "Hm, something's missing."
<v James Heckman>这些测试分数仅能解释个体差异的一小部分——谁成功谁不成功——还有其他未被衡量的因素，甚至在公共政策中未被考虑到的因素，起到了巨大的作用。所以我说，“嗯，少了点什么。”

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:15.050
<v James Heckman>And now, the first impulse is you appeal to astrophysics and you say, ah dark matter. There's something out there in the universe that we're missing. And that dark matter, what could it be?
<v James Heckman>现在，第一反应是你求助于天体物理学，你会说，啊，暗物质。宇宙中有些东西是我们遗漏的。而那暗物质可能是什么？

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:34.770
<v Ira Glass>What were the skills that the GED students lacked that the high school graduates had? Specifically, what was this unnamed dark matter, and how could you measure it? Heckman started calling these mystery skills that he was looking for non-cognitive skills to distinguish them from the stuff that educators normally focused on, which of course were cognitive skills.
<v Ira Glass>GED学生缺乏而高中毕业生拥有的技能是什么？具体来说，这种未命名的暗物质是什么，你如何衡量它？赫克曼开始将他寻找的这些神秘技能称为“非认知技能”，以区别于教育工作者通常关注的内容，也就是认知技能。

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:44.860
<v Ira Glass>But it must be a drag saying the phrase "non-cognitive skills" all the time because when you talk to Heckman, over the course of a conversation, he jumps around to all kinds of other words to describe the same thing.
<v Ira Glass>但是一直说“非认知技能”这个词一定很烦人，因为当你和赫克曼交谈时，在对话过程中，他会跳跃到各种其他词汇来描述同样的事情。

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.230
<v James Heckman>Soft skills.
<v James Heckman>软技能。

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.390
<v Ira Glass>Or a moment later.
<v Ira Glass>或者是稍后提到的。

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:48.750
<v James Heckman>Social skills.
<v James Heckman>社交技能。

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:50.060
<v Ira Glass>Or here's another.
<v Ira Glass>或者还有另一个。

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:51.870
<v James Heckman>Personality traits.
<v James Heckman>人格特质。

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:55.670
<v Ira Glass>You know what you guys need is you need a better word for this.
<v Ira Glass>你知道你们需要的是一个更好的词。

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.110
<v James Heckman>Better word? "Non-cognitive" is not good, you mean?
<v James Heckman>更好的词？你是说“非认知”不好吗？

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:03.220
<v Ira Glass>No. The phrase "non-cognitive skills," it just feels a little confusing or clumsy.
<v Ira Glass>不。“非认知技能”这个词感觉有点令人困惑或笨拙。

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:06.240
<v James Heckman>I agree with you. It's lousy. It sounds like a very negative terminology.
<v James Heckman>我同意你的看法。它很糟糕。听起来像是一个非常消极的术语。

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:12.660
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's funny talking to you about it. Because even as you talk about, I hear you run through all the different terms, because none seems to suit.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，和你讨论这个很有趣。因为即使你在谈论它时，我也听到你用了各种不同的词，因为似乎没有一个词是合适的。

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:13.330
<v James Heckman>Correct.
<v James Heckman>没错。

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.600
<v Ira Glass>Because you guys haven't come up with a term yet.
<v Ira Glass>因为你们还没想出一个术语。

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:28.670
<v James Heckman>Well, no, we have-- character. Character. The trouble is "character" sounds very moralistic. It sounds like we're running a Sunday School. See, I think the proper word is probably personality and social skills.
<v James Heckman>嗯，不，我们有——品格。品格。麻烦的是“品格”听起来非常道德说教。听起来像是在办主日学。你看，我认为合适的词可能是个性和社交技能。

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:32.140
<v Ira Glass>Listen to you! You're changing from minute to minute.
<v Ira Glass>听听你说的！你每分钟都在变。

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.000
<v James Heckman>I agree, I agree. We go all over it.
<v James Heckman>我同意，我同意。我们一直在反复讨论。

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:56.970
<v Ira Glass>Heckman says it's not like educators never considered character before this-- or should I say non-cognitive skills? The history of American schools is a pendulum that swings between emphasizing character on the one hand and intelligence on the other. Everything from classical boarding schools to progressive schools to modern private schools have endeavored to teach character, not just the ABCs.
<v Ira Glass>赫克曼说，这并不是说教育工作者在这之前从未考虑过品格——或者我应该说非认知技能？美国学校的历史就像一个钟摆，在一端强调品格，在另一端强调智力。从古典寄宿学校到进步学校再到现代私立学校，一切都在努力教授品格，而不仅仅是ABC。

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:15.350
<v Ira Glass>And public schools once taught character-- conduct, deportment. Those are the words they used, which Heckman would call non-cognitive skills. But over the course of the 20th century, testing-- standardized testing of math and reading and other cognitive skills-- grew and it grew until it dominated American education.
<v Ira Glass>公立学校曾经教导品格——行为举止。那是他们使用的词，赫克曼会称之为非认知技能。但到了20世纪，测试——数学和阅读以及其他认知技能的标准化测试——不断增长，直到它统治了美国教育。

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:32.240
<v James Heckman>What happened-- and this is just the logic of testing, how tests have a life of their own. But literally what happened was it became very easy to mark reading, writing tests much harder to judge deportment, to judge all these other traits. It's hard as hell to measure those.
<v James Heckman>发生了什么——这只是测试的逻辑，测试是如何拥有自己生命的。但实际上发生的是，评估阅读和写作测试变得非常容易，而判断品格、判断所有这些其他特质则困难得多。衡量这些东西难如登天。

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:43.840
<v Ira Glass>And so most schools gradually organized themselves around teaching stuff that you can measure, stuff you can quantify in test results, which are the cognitive skills. All this other stuff went by the wayside.
<v Ira Glass>所以大多数学校逐渐围绕着教授那些你可以衡量的东西来组织自己，那些可以在测试结果中量化的东西，也就是认知技能。所有这些其他东西都被抛弃了。

00:13:43.840 --> 00:14:05.790
<v Ira Glass>Now, Heckman wanted to take a look at it anew. And specifically, he was interested in finding skills that he could prove, empirically prove, help kids succeed-- and succeed in the ways that an economist measure success. Things like how much money do you earn, and do you end up in prison, and are you on welfare? And he then wanted to try to understand how these things could be taught.
<v Ira Glass>现在，赫克曼想重新审视它。具体来说，他有兴趣寻找那些他可以证明、经得起经验证明能够帮助孩子成功的东西——并且是以经济学家衡量成功的方式取得成功。比如你挣多少钱，你是否最终入狱，你是否靠福利生活？然后他想尝试了解这些东西是如何被教授的。

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:21.130
<v Ira Glass>For such a prominent academic, a Nobel Prize winner, to tackle all this and tackle all this in this way was new. Nobody set out on a mission like the one that Heckman now embarked on, pulling in and connecting people who were already working on pieces of the puzzle. Again, Paul Tough.
<v Ira Glass>对于一位如此杰出的学者、诺贝尔奖得主来说，以这种方式处理这一切是全新的。没有人像赫克曼现在所做的那样，设定一个任务，去汇集和联系那些已经在研究拼图碎片的人们。再次重申，保罗·塔夫。

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.210
<v Paul Tough>And that's what he's been doing for the last 10 years.
<v Paul Tough>这就是他过去10年来一直在做的事情。

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:34.050
<v Ira Glass>And so as the research has gone on, what kinds of things have people found are these non-cognitive skills? Like, what are they?
<v Ira Glass>那么随着研究的进行，人们发现什么样的东西是这些非认知技能？比如，它们是什么？

00:14:34.050 --> 00:15:06.280
<v Paul Tough>Well, there's not sort of a master list of non-cognitive skills right now. The ones that Heckman would refer to and that I think are most important, some of them have to do with self-control. There's a number of different terms-- things like self-regulation skills, self-control, conscientiousness. So that, I think, is one set of things. Just the ability to delay gratification, to resist impulse. When you're about to make a bad decision, to think twice about it. To keep your temper. All of those things, I think, matter a lot.
<v Paul Tough>嗯，目前还没有一张非认知技能的清单。赫克曼会提到而且我认为最重要的一些，有些与自我控制有关。有许多不同的术语——比如自我调节技能、自我控制、尽责性。所以，我认为这是一类东西。只是延迟满足的能力，抵制冲动的能力。当你即将做出一个糟糕决定时，三思而行。控制你的脾气。我认为所有这些都很重要。

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:17.230
<v Ira Glass>And I feel like when you say that, it makes me think of studies that I think a lot of us have read about where they'll put a kid into a room. And they have to resist eating a cookie, something like that.
<v Ira Glass>而且我觉得当你这么说时，让我想起了我们很多人读过的研究，他们会把一个孩子关进房间。他们必须抵制吃掉一块饼干，诸如此类的事情。

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:54.630
<v Paul Tough>Yeah, this is called the marshmallow test. And this is a famous test that was done starting back in the 1960s by a psychology professor then at Stanford named Walter Mischel. And he took a bunch of four-year-olds into a room, said, "Here is a marshmallow or a cookie. I'm going to leave the room. If you want, you can just ring this bell, and I'll come back, and I'll give you one of this treat. But if you can wait until I come back on my own, then you get two." And so this was like psychological torture for these kids.
<v Paul Tough>是的，这叫棉花糖测试。这是斯坦福大学一位心理学教授沃尔特·米歇尔（Walter Mischel）从20世纪60年代开始做的一个著名实验。他把一群四岁的孩子带进房间，说：“这有一块棉花糖或饼干。我要离开房间。如果你愿意，你可以按这个铃，我会回来，然后给你一块。但如果你能等到我回来，那你就得到两块。”这简直是对这些孩子的心理折磨。

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:55.400
<v Ira Glass>They were four.
<v Ira Glass>他们才四岁。

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.840
<v Paul Tough>Exactly. And so they would sit there and try to resist ringing this bell.
<v Paul Tough>没错。所以他们会坐在那里试图抵制按下铃的冲动。

00:15:58.840 --> 00:15:59.720
<v Ira Glass>And squirm.
<v Ira Glass>而且坐立不安。

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.250
<v Paul Tough>Exactly. It was very painful for them.
<v Paul Tough>没错。这对他们来说非常痛苦。

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:08.330
<v Theo>[WAILS] It's so long!
<v Theo>[哭喊] 时间太长了！

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:18.520
<v Ira Glass>This is such a well-known study that on YouTube, you can see videos that various parents have made administering this test to their own kids in their own homes.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个如此知名的研究，以至于在YouTube上，你可以看到各种家长在自己家中对孩子进行这个测试的视频。

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:25.310
<v Theo>10 minutes. 10 minutes! 10 minutes! 10 minutes!
<v Theo>10分钟。10分钟！10分钟！10分钟！

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:32.840
<v Ira Glass>This little boy named Theo has obviously been told that he's going to get his second treat if he can just wait 10 minutes. And he sits there.
<v Ira Glass>这个名叫西奥（Theo）的小男孩显然被告知，如果他能等上10分钟，他就会得到第二块零食。于是他坐在那里。

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:47.000
<v Theo>10 minutes! 10 minutes! 10 minutes! 10 minutes! 10 minutes! Aw, 10 minutes!
<v Theo>10分钟！10分钟！10分钟！10分钟！10分钟！哎，10分钟！

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.570
<v Ira Glass>So anyway, back to Walter Mischel.
<v Ira Glass>总之，回到沃尔特·米歇尔。

00:16:49.570 --> 00:17:01.890
<v Paul Tough>He was actually doing this study to try to figure out how kids resisted temptation. And it was only like 10 and 15 and 20 years down the road when he started looking these kids up again, and he wanted to find out how well they were doing.
<v Paul Tough>他其实做这项研究是为了找出孩子们是如何抵制诱惑的。直到10年、15年、20年后，当他开始再次寻找这些孩子，想要了解他们现在过得如何时。

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:21.050
<v Paul Tough>And he found there were these huge correlations between how long these kids had been able to wait for the treat and how well they did on their SATs, how much money they were making, all sorts of indicators of how well they were doing in life.
<v Paul Tough>他发现这些孩子等待零食的时间长短与他们在SAT考试中的表现、他们赚多少钱以及生活中表现良好的各种指标之间存在巨大的相关性。

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:39.150
<v Ira Glass>Now, for James Heckman, and for writer Paul Tough for that matter, what is most exciting about the power of these non-cognitive skills is the possibility they would give kids the skills they need to get out of poverty and the skills they need in the short run to counter-act the effects of poverty on children in school.
<v Ira Glass>现在，对于詹姆斯·赫克曼，以及作者保罗·塔夫来说，这些非认知技能的力量最令人兴奋的是，它们有可能让孩子们获得摆脱贫困所需的技能，以及在短期内抵消贫困对在校儿童影响的技能。

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:47.360
<v Ira Glass>It's well-documented that poor children do worse on tests and worse in school than better-off ones. This is the so-called achievement gap.
<v Ira Glass>有据可查的是，贫困儿童在测试和学校中的表现比富裕家庭的孩子差。这就是所谓的成就差距。

00:17:47.360 --> 00:18:09.370
<v Ira Glass>What this new science seems to indicate is that what is holding these children back is not poverty. It's not the lack of money or resources in their homes. It's stress. If you grew up in a poor household, it is more likely to be a household the just stresses you out in ways that kids in better-off homes are not stressed out. And that stress prevents you from developing these non-cognitive skills.
<v Ira Glass>这项新科学似乎表明，阻碍这些孩子的不是贫困。不是他们家里缺乏金钱或资源。而是压力。如果你在贫困家庭长大，它更有可能是一个让你倍感压力的家庭，而富裕家庭的孩子不会有这种压力。这种压力阻止了你发展这些非认知技能。

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:12.730
<v Nadine Burke Harris>What was stumping me was how does this work.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>让我困惑的是，这到底是怎么运作的。

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:39.410
<v Ira Glass>Let me introduce you to somebody else that Paul Tough writes about in his book. This is Dr. Nadine Burke Harris. In 2007, she set up a clinic for children in Bayview-Hunters Point in San Francisco. And she set it up there because poor, minority communities like that one are often less healthy than other communities. She was an idealistic, young, black doctor. And she wanted to do something about that. She also had a master's degree in public health. But stress and its effects on kids wasn't on her radar yet.
<v Ira Glass>让我为你介绍保罗·塔夫书中提到的另一个人。她是纳丁·伯克·哈里斯（Nadine Burke Harris）医生。2007年，她在旧金山湾景-猎人角（Bayview-Hunters Point）为孩子们开办了一家诊所。她在那里开办诊所，是因为像那样的贫困少数族裔社区通常比其他社区的健康状况要差。她是一位理想主义的年轻黑人医生。她想对此有所作为。她还拥有公共卫生硕士学位。但压力及其对儿童的影响还不在她的视野中。

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:56.270
<v Nadine Burke Harris>I thought that if we kind of did the basic stuff-- if we worked on asthma, hospitalization rates and immunization rates and obesity treatment-- those would be the big public health things that we would need to do to make a difference in this community.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>我原以为，如果我们做一些基础工作——如果我们解决哮喘、住院率、免疫接种率和肥胖症治疗——那将是我们在这个社区做出改变所需的重大公共卫生事项。

00:18:56.270 --> 00:19:13.660
<v Nadine Burke Harris>And what I found in my daily practice was that a lot of folks were sending me kids and saying, you know, "Oh, Dr. Burke, Bobby's got ADHD. Can you put him on Ritalin? Or can you put him on some kind of medication?"
<v Nadine Burke Harris>我在日常实践中发现，很多人送孩子来找我说，“哦，伯克医生，鲍比（Bobby）得了多动症（ADHD）。你能给他开利他林吗？或者你能给他开某种药物吗？”

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:30.700
<v Nadine Burke Harris>But when I did my job and did an actual history and physical, which is what doctors are supposed to do, what I found was most of these kids really didn't have ADHD. And most of these kids had issues associated with trauma.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>但当我完成我的工作，做了医生应该做的病史和身体检查时，我发现这些孩子中的大多数并没有多动症。他们中的大多数有与创伤相关的问题。

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:35.770
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to talk a little bit about the kind of trauma you're talking about. What was going on in their lives?
<v Ira Glass>我想请你谈谈你所说的创伤类型。他们的生活中发生了什么？

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:56.230
<v Nadine Burke Harris>Yeah, so as a pediatrician, I feel like I had the opportunity to hear some of the real story. But we're talking about kids who are witnessing violence on a daily basis. And some of it was domestic violence. Just domestic violence like you wouldn't believe.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>是的，作为儿科医生，我觉得我有机会听到一些真实的故事。但我们谈论的是那些每天都在目睹暴力的孩子。其中一些是家庭暴力。那种你简直无法相信的家庭暴力。

00:19:56.230 --> 00:20:23.740
<v Nadine Burke Harris>I had a family-- you know, sometimes it's almost mundane things, a family who leave the house, and they are boarding up their own windows because they're afraid that their neighbors will come in and rob them. I had a family where, not only did their neighbors rob them, they took everything, including the food in the fridge and the toilet paper off the roll. Right?
<v Nadine Burke Harris>我有个家庭——你知道，有时候这几乎是些平凡的小事，一个家庭离开房子时，他们会把自己的窗户封死，因为他们害怕邻居会进来抢劫他们。我有个家庭，邻居不仅抢劫了他们，还拿走了所有东西，包括冰箱里的食物和卷纸架上的卫生纸。对吧？

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.620
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:29.180
<v Nadine Burke Harris>Someone really robs you when they take your toilet paper.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>当有人拿走你的卫生纸时，那真是抢劫。

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:45.540
<v Ira Glass>She started to believe that there was some connection between the terrible situations these kids were living through and the symptoms they showed up with-- headaches, abdominal pain, difficulty with digestion. One girl's hair started falling out. Some little kids had a medical condition called failure to thrive where they didn't grow well.
<v Ira Glass>她开始相信，这些孩子经历的可怕处境与他们表现出来的症状——头痛、腹痛、消化困难——之间存在某种联系。一个女孩开始掉头发。一些小孩患有一种叫“生长迟缓”的疾病，导致发育不良。

00:20:45.540 --> 00:21:04.730
<v Ira Glass>But nothing in her medical training or her public health training explained this. So she researched till she found this big, authoritative study that followed over 17,000 patients from San Diego HMO that showed that people who had adverse childhood experiences when they were little ended up with worse health that continued into adulthood.
<v Ira Glass>但她的医学培训或公共卫生培训中没有任何东西能解释这一点。所以她深入研究，直到她找到那项覆盖圣地亚哥健康维护组织（HMO）超过17,000名患者的大型权威研究，该研究显示，小时候经历过不利童年经历的人，其健康状况在成年后依然较差。

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:16.150
<v Ira Glass>She says that for her, it was like that scene in The Matrix when Neo suddenly understands everything about the world. And as we talked, she was actually able to rattle off their findings from memory. She said, if you had enough difficult childhood experiences--
<v Ira Glass>她说，这对她来说，就像《黑客帝国》（The Matrix）里尼奥（Neo）突然理解了世界万物的那一幕。当我们交谈时，她实际上能够凭记忆背诵出研究结果。她说，如果你经历过足够多的童年困难——

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:35.960
<v Nadine Burke Harris>You were two and a half times as likely to develop chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in adulthood, twice as likely to have heart disease, two and a half times as likely to have hepatitis, four and a half times as likely to be depressed, 12 times as likely to attempt suicide.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>你成年后患慢性阻塞性肺病的几率是常人的2.5倍，患心脏病的几率是2倍，患肝炎的几率是2.5倍，患抑郁症的几率是4.5倍，尝试自杀的几率是12倍。

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:47.240
<v Ira Glass>But in addition to all the bad things that are likely to happen to them as adults, there's also the effect that long-term stress has on them when they're still kids, especially on their brains and their ability to learn.
<v Ira Glass>但除了成年后可能发生的坏事之外，长期压力对他们尚在童年时的影响也很大，特别是在大脑和学习能力方面。

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:59.930
<v Nadine Burke Harris>If you look on the molecular level, you're walking through the forest and you see a bear, right? So you can either fight the bear or run from the bear. That's kind of your fight or flight system. Right?
<v Nadine Burke Harris>如果你从分子层面看，你在森林里散步，看到了一只熊，对吧？所以你要么打熊，要么从熊身边逃跑。这就是所谓的战斗或逃跑系统。对吧？

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:00.740
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:34.440
<v Nadine Burke Harris>And your body releases a ton of adrenalin, right? Which is your short-term stress hormone, and something else called cortisol, which tends to be more of a long-term stress hormone. And this dilates your pupils, gets your heart beating fast. Your skin gets cold and clammy. That's because you're shunting blood from anywhere that isn't absolutely necessary to the muscles that you need to be able to run from that bear.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>你的身体释放大量的肾上腺素，对吧？这是你的短期压力荷尔蒙，还有另一种叫做皮质醇的东西，往往更偏向于长期压力荷尔蒙。这会使你的瞳孔放大，让心脏快速跳动。你的皮肤变得又冷又粘。那是因为你正在将血液从任何并非绝对必要的地方转移到你逃避那只熊所需的肌肉上。

00:22:34.440 --> 00:23:04.090
<v Nadine Burke Harris>The other thing that it does-- now, you can imagine that if you're about to fight a bear, you need some gumption to fight that bear, right? So it kind of shuts off the thinking portion of your brain, right? That executive function cognitive part. And it turns on the real primal aggression and the things that you need to be able to think that you're going to go into a fight with a bear and come out on the winning side.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>它做的另一件事——现在，你可以想象，如果你要和一只熊搏斗，你需要一些胆量来对抗那只熊，对吧？所以它会关闭你大脑中负责思考的部分，对吧？那种执行功能认知部分。然后它会开启原始的攻击性以及你需要去面对一只熊搏斗并获胜所需的东西。

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.070
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:45.920
<v Nadine Burke Harris>And that's really good if you're in a forest and there's a bear. The problem is when that bear comes home from the bar every night. Right? And for a lot of these kids, what happens is that this system, this fight or flight response, which is an emergency response in your body, it's activated over and over and over again. And so that's what we were seeing in the kids that I was caring for.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>如果你在森林里且有一只熊，那确实很好。问题在于，当那只熊每天晚上从酒吧回家时。对吧？对于很多孩子来说，发生的事情是，这个系统，这个战斗或逃跑反应，作为一个身体的紧急反应，被一遍又一遍地激活。所以这就是我在照顾那些孩子时所看到的。

00:23:45.920 --> 00:24:11.610
<v Ira Glass>When the brain does something over and over and over again, it creates pathways that get more and more ingrained. So this kind of repeated stress affects the development of these kids' brains. And especially affected in this situation is a specific part of the brain that's called the prefrontal cortex, which is where a lot of these non-cognitive skills happen-- self-control and impulse control, certain kinds of memory and reasoning. Skills they call executive functions.
<v Ira Glass>当大脑一遍又一遍地做某件事时，它会创造出越来越根深蒂固的通路。所以这种反复的压力会影响这些孩子的大脑发育。在这种情况下，受影响特别严重的是大脑中被称为前额叶皮层的一部分，这是很多非认知技能发生的地方——自我控制、冲动控制、某些记忆和推理。也就是他们所说的执行功能。

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:29.580
<v Ira Glass>If you're in a constant state of emergency, that part of your brain just doesn't develop the same. Doctors can see the differences on brain scans. Dr. Burke Harris says that for these kids, the bear basically never goes away. They still feel its effects even when they're just trying to sit there quietly in English class.
<v Ira Glass>如果你处于持续的紧急状态，你大脑的那部分发育就不会一样。医生可以通过脑部扫描看到差异。伯克·哈里斯医生说，对于这些孩子来说，那只熊基本上从未消失。即使当他们只是试图在英语课上安静地坐着时，他们仍能感觉到它的影响。

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:53.540
<v Nadine Burke Harris>And if right at that moment someone asks you, "Oh, could you please diagram this sentence? Or could you please divide two complex numbers?" You'd be like, what are you talking about? And so that's what we were seeing in the kids that I was caring for, is that a lot of them had a terrible time paying attention. They have a hard time sitting still.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>如果就在那一刻，有人问你，“哦，你能把这个句子分解一下吗？或者你能除这两个复数吗？”你会觉得，你在说什么？这就是我在照顾那些孩子时所看到的，他们很多人注意力极难集中，很难安稳地坐着。

00:24:53.540 --> 00:25:11.710
<v Ira Glass>And you hear about this in lots of schools. Head Start teachers in one survey said that over a fourth of their low income students had serious self-control and behavior problems. Nadine Burke Harris says that it's true for her patients, the ones with adverse childhood experiences like neglect, domestic violence, a parent with mental illness or substance abuse.
<v Ira Glass>你在很多学校都会听到这种情况。Head Start教师在一项调查中表示，超过四分之一的低收入学生有严重的自我控制和行为问题。纳丁·伯克·哈里斯说，这对她的患者来说也是真实的，那些有忽视、家庭暴力、父母患有精神疾病或药物滥用等不利童年经历的孩子。

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:39.210
<v Nadine Burke Harris>For our kids, if they had four or more adverse childhood experiences, their odds of having learning or behavior problems in school was 32 times as high as kids who had no adverse childhood experiences.
<v Nadine Burke Harris>对于我们的孩子来说，如果他们有四种或更多的不利童年经历，他们在学校出现学习或行为问题的几率是那些没有不利童年经历的孩子的32倍。

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:46.680
<v Ira Glass>Paul Tough writes about a number of teenagers and how this plays out for them. One of them is a girl who he met when she was 17, Kewauna Lerma.
<v Ira Glass>保罗·塔夫写了一些青少年以及这对他们有何影响的事情。其中之一是他17岁时遇到的一个女孩，凯瓦纳·勒玛（Kewauna Lerma）。

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:56.470
<v Paul Tough>She had a really chaotic childhood. Her parents split up. She moved around a lot. She was homeless for a while. Just all the bad things that happen to poor kids were happening to her.
<v Paul Tough>她童年非常混乱。父母分开了。她经常搬家。有一段时间她无家可归。贫困孩子经历的所有坏事都在她身上发生了。

00:25:56.470 --> 00:26:01.050
<v Kewauna Lerma>The first 10 years of my life was really messed up.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我生命的前10年真的是一团糟。

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:05.550
<v Ira Glass>This is Kewauna. She came into the studio for this radio story to talk with me and Paul.
<v Ira Glass>这就是凯瓦纳。她来到演播室，和我和保罗谈论这个广播故事。

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:20.420
<v Kewauna Lerma>Because it was like my mom, for five years, she was going to place to place, sleeping wherever she could sleep at, you know. And when I was born, my mom and my father, they really couldn't take care of another child or whatever.
<v Kewauna Lerma>因为就像我妈妈，五年间，她居无定所，能在哪睡就在哪睡，你知道。当我出生时，我的父母真的无法照顾另一个孩子之类的。

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:25.360
<v Kewauna Lerma>It's so many different incidents I remember. It's hard to state on one.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我记得很多不同的事件。很难只挑一件说。

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:31.010
<v Ira Glass>When you think back on those times, do you think of that as like, you were mostly happy most of the time? What was it like for you?
<v Ira Glass>当你回想起那些时光时，你会认为那是你大部分时间都很快乐吗？那对你来说是什么感觉？

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:43.910
<v Kewauna Lerma>No, I was never happy. I was never happy, because I was just an angry child. And I really don't understand what I was angry about. I can't even remember what I was angry about.
<v Kewauna Lerma>不，我从来不快乐。我从来不快乐，因为我只是个愤怒的孩子。我真的不明白我在为什么愤怒。我甚至不记得我在为什么愤怒了。

00:26:43.910 --> 00:27:05.820
<v Paul Tough>And so she had trouble in all kinds of ways. And the two biggest ways that she had trouble were ones that, I think anyway, had a lot to do with her brain development. One is that she had a really bad temper, bad impulse control. She would just kind of shut down. And she couldn't see anything. And she would just go into these rages. And that was a real problem, especially in school.
<v Paul Tough>所以她在各方面都有问题。而她遇到的两个最大的问题，我想 anyway，与她的大脑发育有很大关系。一是她的脾气非常暴躁，冲动控制能力很差。她会直接关闭自己，什么也看不见，然后陷入这种愤怒中。这真的是个大问题，特别是在学校。

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:09.880
<v Kewauna Lerma>I can't even remember this particular incident when we was in that classroom.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我甚至不记得我们在那间教室里的那次特定事件。

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:17.430
<v Ira Glass>Kewauna got into lots of fights. For instance, with one girl  at school who got to her, Kewauna told me, "like a bug crawling under your sweater."
<v Ira Glass>凯瓦纳经常打架。例如，学校里的一个女孩惹恼了她，凯瓦纳告诉我，“就像一只虫子爬进你的毛衣里。”

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:33.670
<v Kewauna Lerma>One time I did that, she just made me so mad, I just started screaming everything I could in class. I just got up like I was a terminator or something. And I was about to attack her. And I was like screaming. And I just like stopped the whole classroom or whatever. And Mr. Coleman--
<v Kewauna Lerma>有一次我确实那样做了，她让我太生气了，我就开始在课堂上大喊大叫。我就像终结者什么的站起来。我正要攻击她。我尖叫着。我就像让整个课堂停了下来什么的。然后科尔曼老师——

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:35.720
<v Ira Glass>This is the dean of her high school, Mr. Coleman.
<v Ira Glass>这是她的高中主任，科尔曼老师。

00:27:35.720 --> 00:28:01.230
<v Kewauna Lerma>He told me, "Miss Lerma, stop cussing." But when I blank out, I'm not thinking about nobody that's right here in front of me. I see him in front of me just doing this. And he told me about that. He was like, you blanked out. It was like you didn't see me. He was like, I was saying, "Miss Lerma, stop cussing. And you just kept on cussing." And I was like, I don't know. I blank out.
<v Kewauna Lerma>他告诉我，“勒玛小姐，停止辱骂。”但当我断片的时候，我根本没在想眼前的人是谁。我看到他在我面前做这个动作。他告诉我那件事。他说，你断片了。就像你根本没看见我一样。他说，我说，“勒玛小姐，停止辱骂。而你一直不停地辱骂。”我说，我不知道。我断片了。

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:05.040
<v Ira Glass>She was one of those kids who was always fighting with teachers, talking back--
<v Ira Glass>她是那种总是和老师打架、顶嘴的孩子——

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:23.090
<v Paul Tough>She told me that, I think, in her sixth grade year, she got 72 referrals, meaning she was sent to the principal 72 times. And as a result, she was sent into the slow class. She was put into this class where all the kids do poorly.
<v Paul Tough>她告诉我，我想是在她六年级的时候，她被转介了72次，意味着她被送去校长办公室72次。结果，她被送进了慢班。她被放进了所有成绩不好的孩子都在的那种班。

00:28:23.090 --> 00:28:24.570
<v Ira Glass>It was called the Wings program?
<v Ira Glass>那叫“翅膀”项目（Wings program）吗？

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:34.590
<v Paul Tough>Exactly, the Wings program. And she still talks about how it was kind of devastating for her emotionally to be put in that class.
<v Paul Tough>没错，“翅膀”项目。她仍然提到被放进那个班级对她在情感上有多么摧毁。

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:37.770
<v Kewauna Lerma>So I got put in this program called Wings, and I was embarrassed.
<v Kewauna Lerma>所以我被放进了一个叫“翅膀”的项目，我很尴尬。

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:40.530
<v Ira Glass>You were embarrassed because you just felt like?
<v Ira Glass>你感到尴尬是因为你只是觉得？

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:55.160
<v Kewauna Lerma>Because we were retarded. That's was we was called. It wasn't like-- I'm not going to beat around a bush. I'm not going to say like, "we were just under-performing." No. We were retarded. That's what we got called. That's what we were seen as. That's what it was.
<v Kewauna Lerma>因为我们是白痴（retarded）。那就是我们被叫的名字。我不是——我不会兜圈子。我不会说“我们只是表现不佳”。不。我们是白痴。那就是我们被叫的。那就是我们被视为的样子。事实就是这样。

00:28:55.160 --> 00:29:02.670
<v Paul Tough>And was the idea in Wings that you were supposed to just go there for a while and get your remedial stuff and then get back in your regular class? What were they doing in Wings?
<v Paul Tough>那么在“翅膀”里的想法是你应该去那里待一段时间，补习一下基础课，然后回到常规班级吗？他们在“翅膀”里做什么？

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:39.230
<v Kewauna Lerma>We wasn't doing nothing. That's what they said we were going to be doing. But most times, we would play video games, eat popcorn. Nobody never did-- we didn't do anything. It was like they were just carrying us to our tables every day. I felt like they treated us like something was wrong with us or something. They were like, "You want to play the video game? Here you go. Let's play a video game." I can't do that in my other class, so why are we doing it here, you know?
<v Kewauna Lerma>我们什么都没做。他们说我们会做点什么。但大多数时候，我们就是打电子游戏、吃爆米花。没人——我们什么也没做。感觉他们每天只是把我们带到桌子旁。我觉得他们对待我们就像我们有毛病一样。他们会说，“想玩电子游戏吗？给你。我们玩游戏吧。”我在其他班级做不到，那为什么我们在这里要做这个呢，你知道？

00:29:39.230 --> 00:29:42.790
<v Ira Glass>Paul Tough says that what happened to Kewauna happens to kids all the time.
<v Ira Glass>保罗·塔夫说，凯瓦纳身上发生的事一直在孩子们身上重演。

00:29:42.790 --> 00:30:02.230
<v Paul Tough>Where the early stresses and problems that had to do with her family and had to do with how her parents were dealing with life all were creating in her brain a set of problems that, then when she got into the school system, they didn't have a way to deal with it except for putting her into the slow class, except for saying, "You're a problem child."
<v Paul Tough>早期与她家庭相关的压力和问题，以及她父母处理生活的方式，都在她大脑中制造了一系列问题，然后当她进入学校系统时，他们除了把她放进慢班，除了说“你是个问题儿童”之外，别无他法。

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:08.430
<v Ira Glass>Which raises the question, what else could the school system do for a kid like this? What will work?
<v Ira Glass>这就提出了一个问题，学校系统还能为这样的孩子做什么？什么会有效？

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:24.790
<v Ira Glass>Well, James Heckman, the economist you heard earlier who's studying these non-cognitive skills, says that one advantage of working with non-cognitive skills is that they can be learned. That's not true for cognitive ability, for intelligence the way we usually think of it, like your intelligence on an IQ test.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，刚才你听到的那位研究非认知技能的经济学家詹姆斯·赫克曼说，处理非认知技能的一个优势是它们是可以被学习的。这对于认知能力，对于我们通常理解的智力，比如你在智商测试中的智力，是不适用的。

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:36.580
<v James Heckman>Well cognitive, pure IQ tends to be pretty hardened, at least what's called rank stability. If you're a top dog in the distribution at age 10, you're probably going to be top dog at age 30.
<v James Heckman>纯粹的认知智商往往相当僵化，至少是所谓的排名稳定性。如果你在10岁时处于排名的顶端，你可能在30岁时依然处于顶端。

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:38.610
<v Ira Glass>Versus non-cognitive skills, he says.
<v Ira Glass>对比非认知技能，他说。

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:59.120
<v James Heckman>Social skills, personality traits, the ability to stay on task-- these can be taught. And these can be taught at later ages. And there's a malleability there that actually offers a new perspective on social policy-- how social policy might redirect itself towards those more malleable soft skills.
<v James Heckman>社交技能、人格特质、保持任务的能力——这些是可以教授的。而且可以在较晚的年龄段教授。这具有一定的可塑性，实际上为社会政策提供了新的视角——社会政策可能如何重新引导向那些更具可塑性的软技能。

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:22.080
<v Ira Glass>And so with that in mind, programs and experiments have sprung up to try to change kids' lives by teaching these non-cognitive skills. And Paul Tough documents a bunch of these in his book. What's striking, Tough says, is how simple some of the interventions are. Many of them basically come down to having a coach for kids to teach them resilience and optimism and grit in certain situations.
<v Ira Glass>考虑到这一点，各种旨在通过教授这些非认知技能来改变孩子生活的项目和实验如雨后春笋般涌现。保罗·塔夫在他的书中记录了许多这类项目。塔夫说，引人注目的是，其中一些干预措施竟然如此简单。它们中很多基本上归结为为孩子们提供教练，教他们在特定情况下保持韧性、乐观和坚毅。

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:47.740
<v Paul Tough>There are some studies where they've taken kids and given them-- taken kids, I think, in middle school and given them a college mentor. And this college mentor would work with these kids not very intensively, like meeting with them a couple of times a year. And the only thing that they would say to them, the only message that they would give to them, is simply this idea that, "You know, scientists have studied intelligence. And they've found that you can improve your intelligence. So you should just think about that as you're going through your day."
<v Paul Tough>有一些研究，他们挑选了孩子，为他们提供——我想是初中的孩子，给他们找了一个大学生导师。这位大学生导师并没有非常频繁地与这些孩子互动，比如一年见他们几次。而他们唯一会告诉他们的信息，唯一传递的信息就是这个简单的想法：“你知道，科学家们研究过智力。他们发现你可以提高你的智力。所以你在度过一天时，就这么想吧。”

00:31:47.740 --> 00:32:04.390
<v Paul Tough>Whether or not they're right that they can improve their intelligence, if you think that, especially when you're a kid, when you're a teenager, you do way better at school. It gives you this sort of confidence, this optimism, this ability to try new things, to push yourself harder.
<v Paul Tough>不管他们说能提高智力是对是错，如果你这么想，特别是当你还是个孩子、还是个少年的时候，你在学校的表现就会好得多。这给了你一种自信、乐观，以及尝试新事物、更加鞭策自己的能力。

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:16.230
<v Paul Tough>And one of the things that I find most interesting about this study is that it's the kids who have sort of cultural reasons to be anxious about their skills who are most affected by the message.
<v Paul Tough>而在我看来，这项研究中最有趣的一点是，那些在文化上容易对自己的技能感到焦虑的孩子，受这种信息的影响最大。

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:34.020
<v Paul Tough>So especially girls and math-- you know, girls do great on math until they get to middle school. And then they start becoming socially anxious. And they tend to fall apart. When they hear this message, the girls catch up to the boys entirely. They close that gap 100%.
<v Paul Tough>所以特别是女孩和数学——你知道，女孩在初中之前数学都很好。然后她们开始产生社交焦虑。她们往往会崩溃。当她们听到这个信息时，女孩们完全赶上了男孩。她们100%弥补了差距。

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:50.050
<v Ira Glass>100%, meaning the girls' average math test scores were the same as the boys' average math test scores. That is a huge result. Another example of huge payback for a small amount of coaching happens in early childhood. And this one is an intervention with the parents, not with the kids.
<v Ira Glass>100%，意味着女孩的平均数学测试分数与男孩的平均数学测试分数相同。这是一个巨大的成果。在幼儿时期，少量的辅导也能带来巨大回报的另一个例子。这是一个针对家长而非孩子的干预措施。

00:32:50.050 --> 00:33:04.740
<v Ira Glass>And to explain this intervention, I'm just going to take a little detour here and tell you about a study that involves rats-- stay with me on this one-- involving rat babies and rat parents. This was done at McGill University Again, here's Paul Tough.
<v Ira Glass>为了解释这种干预，我在这里稍微绕个道，告诉你一个关于老鼠的实验——坚持住，听我说完——涉及鼠宝宝和鼠父母。这同样是在麦吉尔大学（McGill University）完成的。再次请出保罗·塔夫。

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:31.660
<v Paul Tough>What they discovered is that there were certain mother rats called dams, who, in their laboratory, tended to lick and groom their pups more, especially in stressful situations. And for a rat pup, the stressful situation in life, if you're in a lab, is getting picked up by a graduate student, by a scientist. And this is really stressful, understandably, for a rat pup.
<v Paul Tough>他们发现有一些母鼠，在实验室里，它们往往会更多地舔舐和梳理幼崽，特别是在压力大的情况下。对于鼠宝宝来说，如果是在实验室里，生活中的压力情境就是被研究生、被科学家捡起来。这对于鼠宝宝来说确实压力很大，这是可以理解的。

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:44.540
<v Paul Tough>And when they put the rat pups back in the cage, certain rat moms would do this ritual of kind of licking and grooming and soothing their rat pups. And certain moms wouldn't
<v Paul Tough>当他们把鼠宝宝放回笼子里时，一些鼠妈妈会进行那种舔舐、梳理和安抚鼠宝宝的仪式。而一些妈妈则不会。

00:33:44.540 --> 00:33:56.820
<v Ira Glass>The researchers wanted to understand what this was about. It seemed significant in some way. So they studied the pups when they grew into adulthood. And they found that the rats that had been licked and groomed were very different from the ones that hadn't been.
<v Ira Glass>研究人员想了解这是关于什么的。它看起来在某种程度上很重要。所以他们研究了那些长大的老鼠。他们发现被舔舐和梳理过的老鼠与那些没有被这样对待过的老鼠有很大不同。

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:17.870
<v Paul Tough>They were more confident. They were better at mazes. They were less fearful. They just did better on all of these different measurements. And what was so interesting and weird about it is that the licking and grooming only happened in the first couple of weeks of life. And then they didn't see their parents at all. And there was this huge difference.
<v Paul Tough>它们更自信。它们跑迷宫更出色。它们更不胆怯。它们在所有这些不同的衡量标准下都表现更好。有趣且古怪的是，舔舐和梳理只发生在生命的最初几周。然后它们根本看不到父母了。但这产生了巨大的差异。

00:34:17.870 --> 00:34:30.350
<v Paul Tough>So it really demonstrated two things. One is the power of parenting-- for rats, at least. And the other was how what really matters is early on, at least in a rat's childhood.
<v Paul Tough>所以这确实证明了两件事。一是育儿的力量——至少对老鼠而言。另一件事是真正重要的，至少在老鼠的童年时期，是早期的经历。

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.060
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:51.220
<v Paul Tough>And so what this connected with for a lot of scientists who were more interested in human babies than rat babies, was the research that scientists have done about attachment. This idea that certain parents are able, especially in the first year of a child's life, to form what's called a secure attachment relationship.
<v Paul Tough>所以对于许多对人类婴儿比对鼠宝宝更感兴趣的科学家来说，这与科学家们关于依恋的研究联系在了一起。这个想法是，有些父母，特别是在孩子生命的第一年，能够形成所谓的安全依恋关系。

00:34:51.220 --> 00:35:01.310
<v Ira Glass>And this is something a lot of parents have heard of. Attachment is an idea that's been around since the 1950s and '60s. What scientists mean when they talk about it has to do with responsiveness, a parent who--
<v Ira Glass>这是很多父母都听说过的事情。依恋是一个自20世纪50年代和60年代以来就存在的想法。当科学家谈论它时，意思是响应性，一个家长能够——

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:20.650
<v Paul Tough>Reacts to how the child is feeling and helps and comforts them, kind of the human equivalent of licking and grooming, helps when the child is stressed out. That tends to create a secure attachment.
<v Paul Tough>对孩子的感受做出反应，帮助和安慰他们，就像人类版本的舔舐和梳理，在孩子感到压力时提供帮助。这往往能建立安全依恋。

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:41.100
<v Ira Glass>Researchers say that about 2/3 of American kids have a secure attachment, 1/3 don't. And the 2/3 with a secure attachment are more socially competent and confident through their lives. They're better at dealing with other people and making friends. They're better able to deal with setbacks. They're more likely to be engaged in the classroom. They're more likely to graduate high school.
<v Ira Glass>研究人员表示，大约三分之二的美国儿童有安全依恋，三分之一没有。那三分之二有安全依恋的孩子在生活中社交能力更强、更自信。他们更擅长与人交往和交朋友。他们更善于应对挫折。他们更有可能参与课堂活动。他们更有可能高中毕业。

00:35:41.100 --> 00:36:15.690
<v Ira Glass>But on top of all these good things that come from attachment, it also has a profound impact on what we're talking about here today-- on non-cognitive skills. If a kid lives in a stressful, chaotic home-- one of those homes where things are bad enough that it can affect their brain development and they don't learn non-cognitive skills-- if kids in that situation have a secure attachment with an empathetic parent, when researchers measured their allostatic load-- the allostatic load is a medical assessment of the effect on their body of stress. They look at blood pressure. They look at the stress hormones in their system and some other factors. What they find is--
<v Ira Glass>但除了依恋带来的所有这些好处外，它对我们今天在这里讨论的内容——非认知技能——也有深远的影响。如果一个孩子生活在充满压力和混乱的家庭中——那种情况糟糕到足以影响他们大脑发育，且他们无法学到非认知技能的家庭——如果这些孩子能与一位有同理心的家长建立安全依恋关系，当研究人员衡量他们的“总负荷”（allostatic load）时——总负荷是对压力给身体造成影响的一种医学评估。他们观察血压。他们观察系统中的压力荷尔蒙以及其他一些因素。他们发现——

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:25.850
<v Paul Tough>That the ones who had that sort of licking and grooming style connection, they didn't have all of those biological effects of early stress. They were mostly OK.
<v Paul Tough>那些拥有那种舔舐和梳理式连接的孩子，并没有表现出早期压力的所有生物学影响。他们大多没问题。

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:27.530
<v Ira Glass>So what does that tell us?
<v Ira Glass>那么这告诉了我们什么？

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:49.580
<v Paul Tough>Well, I think what it tells us is that early stress matters a lot, but that parenting is able to reverse a lot of the effects where they can still be in poverty. They can still be in a pretty stressful environment. But if they have a close relationship with their mother or some other adult, it protects them from the effects of that stuff.
<v Paul Tough>嗯，我认为它告诉我们的是，早期压力非常重要，但育儿能够逆转很多影响，他们仍然可能身处贫困之中。他们仍然可能身处压力很大的环境中。但如果他们与母亲或其他成年人有亲密的联系，这能保护他们免受那些东西的影响。

00:36:49.580 --> 00:37:12.110
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is just science backing up what common sense would tell you anyway. Though there definitely comes a point in reading Paul Tough's book when he's discussing how attachment can protect a kid from the effects of a chaotic home or a bad neighborhood that it is really hard not to think, couldn't somebody just teach this to parents? Parents who don't know how to attach to their kids? Well, it turns out  the answer's yes, and people are doing it.
