00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:48.910
<v Ira Glass>Well, let's say that nobody put your parents into a program like Ounce of Prevention and you grow up in a stressful home without acquiring these non-cognitive skills. It's affected your brain development. Now, you're a teenager. What can be done for you?
<v Ira Glass>那么，假设没有人把你的父母送进像“盎司预防基金会”这样的项目，而你在一个压力巨大的家庭中长大，没有获得这些非认知技能。这影响了你的大脑发育。现在，你是个少年。你能做什么？

00:42:48.910 --> 00:43:02.110
<v Ira Glass>Well, the answer is lots. And like I said earlier, it's amazing how simple some of these interventions are. We will get to them in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，答案是很多。正如我之前所说，这些干预措施简单得令人惊叹。我们稍后会提到，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:22.180
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Today, our Back to School show. We're looking at what children need to learn and talking about this new book by Paul Tough, which makes the case that a whole area of skills called non-cognitive skills are just as important to a child's success as the cognitive skills that our schools are organized around and evaluated on today.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。今天，我们的开学特别节目。我们正在审视孩子们需要学习什么，并讨论保罗·塔夫的新书，该书指出，一整类被称为非认知技能的能力对于孩子的成功和学校目前围绕并进行评估的认知技能一样重要。

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:37.850
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at the second half of our show where we turn back to teenagers and back to Kewauna Lerma who you heard from earlier. Paul Tough actually met Kewauna because she caught a lucky break and ended up in a program that was working with Chicago students and coaching them to learn non-cognitive skills.
<v Ira Glass>我们进入了节目的后半段，我们回到青少年，回到你刚才听到的凯瓦纳·勒玛。保罗·塔夫实际上是遇到了凯瓦纳，因为她幸运地进入了一个项目，该项目致力于与芝加哥学生合作，辅导他们学习非认知技能。

00:43:37.850 --> 00:43:50.290
<v Ira Glass>But before a program like that could do her much good, there was a much more traditional intervention by her family. It came when she was 15. She got arrested for punching a cop. Yes, punching a cop.
<v Ira Glass>但在这样一个项目能对她产生多大帮助之前，她的家庭进行了更传统的干预。那发生在她15岁时。她因为殴打警察被捕。是的，殴打警察。

00:43:50.290 --> 00:44:03.020
<v Ira Glass>She was shorter than five and a half feet. She was 15 years old. She was so aggressive that he called for backup, and they tased her before throwing her into jail. When she got out, her mom took her to her great grandmother's house for a talk.
<v Ira Glass>她身高不到五英尺六英寸。她15岁。她太具有攻击性了，警察不得不请求增援，并在把她扔进监狱前对她使用了泰瑟枪。当她出来时，她妈妈带她去曾祖母家谈话。

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:28.660
<v Kewauna Lerma>So they sat me down. When my grandmother began to talk, that's what kind of made me a little soft, because I'm really sensitive for my great grandmother, because she was the one you could talk to about anything. It was just the main thing that got to me is when it was like tears in the room and stuff. That when I went "Oh, no." Because I'm not good with people that's crying and stuff.
<v Kewauna Lerma>所以她们让我坐下。当我曾祖母开始说话时，那让我有点变软了，因为我对曾祖母真的很敏感，因为她是可以和你谈论任何事的人。最主要的是当房间里有眼泪什么的时，我真的受不了。我当时想“哦，不。”因为我不擅长面对哭泣的人。

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:29.570
<v Ira Glass>They started crying?
<v Ira Glass>她们开始哭了？

00:44:29.570 --> 00:44:56.010
<v Kewauna Lerma>Yeah, my mom. When my mom started crying, she just said, "I don't want you to be like me. I don't want you to go through what I had to go through, the mistakes that I had, the suffering, the home to home, the homeless." Because at a point in my time, we were homeless when my father had left us. He was the income. So we became homeless, and he didn't care.
<v Kewauna Lerma>是的，我妈妈。当我妈妈开始哭时，她说：“我不希望你像我一样。我不希望你经历我不得不经历的事，我不希望你犯我犯过的错误，经历那种苦难，流离失所，无家可归。”因为有一段时间，当父亲离开我们时，我们无家可归。他是家庭收入来源。所以我们变得无家可归，而他根本不在乎。

00:44:56.010 --> 00:45:28.690
<v Kewauna Lerma>My mom was just talking about, like, "I don't want you to be like me. And I don't want you to go through this stuff I have to go through with my kids, not being able to support my kids." And she said the main thing she wanted me to do also was to listen, because that's something that she didn't do. She didn't listen to her mother. And now, look.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我妈妈当时只是说，“我不希望你像我一样。我不希望你经历我不得不经历的养育孩子的事，无法支持我的孩子。”她说她最希望我做的事也是去倾听，因为那是她没做到的事。她没有听她母亲的话。现在，看看吧。

00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:35.410
<v Kewauna Lerma>It was really sincere. It really got through to me, which is the reason why I'm here today.
<v Kewauna Lerma>这真的很真诚。它真的触动了我，这就是为什么我今天能坐在这里的原因。

00:45:35.410 --> 00:45:43.940
<v Ira Glass>The changes didn't happen all at once. But in her sophomore year in high school, Kewauna started doing her homework. She stopped skipping class. She didn't hang out with the same people.
<v Ira Glass>改变并非一蹴而就。但在高中二年级时，凯瓦纳开始做家庭作业了。她不再逃课。她不再和以前那些人混在一起。

00:45:43.940 --> 00:46:07.900
<v Ira Glass>Freshman year before this talk, her GPA was 1.8. Sophomore year, it was 3.4. And then in her sophomore year, she happened to be at a school where she could sign up for a program called One Goal, which works in 23 high schools in Chicago. It's about 1,300 kids. Not in magnet schools or charter programs, these kids are not the cream of the crop. These are average kids in regular schools that do not send many kids to college. Again, Paul Tough.
<v Ira Glass>谈话前的高一，她的平均成绩（GPA）是1.8。高二，变成了3.4。然后在高二时，她碰巧在一所学校，可以报名参加一个叫“一个目标”（One Goal）的项目，该项目在芝加哥的23所高中运作。大约有1300个孩子。不是磁力学校或特许学校，这些孩子不是精英。他们是普通学校里的普通孩子，这些学校没有多少学生能上大学。再次重申，保罗·塔夫。

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:37.470
<v Paul Tough>And they're very much using the research into non-cognitive skills to figure out an intervention for these kids, to help kids really explicitly develop these non-cognitive skills. The term they use is leadership principles-- ambition, professionalism, resourcefulness, resilience-- that they just spend a couple years talking to these kids about and explaining to them how it's going to be important in college.
<v Paul Tough>他们非常利用对非认知技能的研究来为这些孩子设计干预措施，以帮助孩子真正明确地发展这些非认知技能。他们使用的术语是领导力原则——雄心、职业素养、足智多谋、韧性——他们花几年时间与这些孩子讨论，并向他们解释这些在大学里会有多重要。

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:52.240
<v Ira Glass>Now, you made some recordings when you were writing your book of the class. And I should say, these weren't broadcast quality. You were just making them to keep them for yourself. But they're good enough that you can actually hear what's happening. So here's one of those.
<v Ira Glass>现在，你在写书时录了一些课上的录音。我应该说，这些不是广播质量。你只是录下来给自己保留。但它们效果不错，实际上你能听到发生了什么。所以这里是其中一个。

00:46:52.240 --> 00:47:00.700
<v Michelle Steffel>This is the part where you need to start being resourceful. It can't be, "Mrs. Steffel, can you call?" You are making the phone call. I want to make sure that's clear.
<v Michelle Steffel>这是你需要开始变得足智多谋的部分。不能总是，“斯特费尔夫人，你能打个电话吗？”是你自己在打电话。我想确保这一点很清楚。

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:19.290
<v Paul Tough>So what you're hearing is Kewauna's teacher, a woman named Michelle Steffel. There are lots of things that kids are learning in this class. But in some ways, when I would sit in the One Goal classroom, it felt like this kind of ongoing group therapy session. I don't think they would want me to say that, because it sounds too--
<v Paul Tough>所以你听到的是凯瓦纳的老师，一位叫米歇尔·斯特费尔（Michelle Steffel）的女士。孩子们在这个班里学的东西很多。但在某种程度上，当我坐在“一个目标”的教室里时，感觉就像这种持续不断的团体治疗。我不认为他们会想让我这么说，因为它听起来太——

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:19.710
<v Ira Glass>Touchy feely.
<v Ira Glass>感性了。

00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:30.670
<v Paul Tough>Touchy feely. Yeah. But really, that is what makes a huge difference for these kids is learning these psychological skills. And when they get to college, that's what's going to make a difference.
<v Paul Tough>感性。是的。但说真的，这就是这些孩子巨大的区别所在——学习这些心理技能。当他们上大学时，这就是将产生影响的地方。

00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:43.540
<v Michelle Steffel>It's OK to be scared. It's OK to kind of stumble and fall along the way. And you should. It's not going to be easy. It shouldn't be easy. This is your life, OK? It's not going to be a bed of roses. OK?
<v Michelle Steffel>害怕没关系。一路上跌跌撞撞也是正常的。而且你应该如此。这不会容易。也不应该容易。这是你的人生，好吗？生活不会是一床玫瑰。好吗？

00:47:43.540 --> 00:48:05.100
<v Ira Glass>One Goal's premise is it's going to be difficult to make up for how far behind these kids are in their cognitive skills, in traditional test scores and all that. When Kewauna first took a practice ACT test for college, she scored an 11, putting her in the bottom one percentile of everyone who took the ACT that year. After months of study, she said she went up one point.
<v Ira Glass>“一个目标”的前提是，弥补这些孩子在认知技能、传统考试成绩等方面的落后是很难的。当凯瓦纳第一次参加大学ACT练习考试时，她得了11分，让她处于当年所有参加ACT考试的人的后1%。经过几个月的学习，她说她提高了一分。

00:48:05.100 --> 00:48:22.750
<v Ira Glass>But One Goal could teach her non-cognitive skills-- how to be resourceful, how to rebound from a setback, how to control herself and not get into fights-- plus concrete skills that would be useful, like how to present yourself, how to network. Kewauna never used to ask questions in class. She thought her questions were dumb. So she had to learn how to do that.
<v Ira Glass>但“一个目标”可以教她非认知技能——如何足智多谋、如何从挫折中反弹、如何自我控制而不打架——再加上会有用的具体技能，比如如何展示自己、如何建立人脉。凯瓦纳以前从不在课堂上提问。她认为她的问题很蠢。所以她必须学习如何做到这一点。

00:48:22.750 --> 00:48:34.120
<v Ira Glass>She ended up with an ACT score of 15, which, combined with a 4.1 grade point average in her junior and senior years, got her into college-- Western Illinois University in Macomb. She was a freshman last year.
<v Ira Glass>她最终ACT得了15分，加上高三和高四4.1的GPA，这让她进入了大学——马科姆（Macomb）的西伊利诺伊大学。去年她是大一新生。

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:47.360
<v Ira Glass>Paul Tough has stayed in touch with her. And he says her training paid off. In fact, he says it's hard to imagine how she would have gotten to college and made it through freshman year without that training. Non-cognitive traits like grit and self-control are even more important in college than in high school.
<v Ira Glass>保罗·塔夫一直与她保持联系。他说她的训练得到了回报。事实上，他说很难想象如果没有那种训练，她会如何考上大学并度过大一。像坚毅和自我控制这样的非认知特质在大学里比在高中更重要。

00:48:47.360 --> 00:49:09.090
<v Paul Tough>And then the other thing that I think has made a huge difference for her at college especially is this resourcefulness, is this sense that she has of like, "I'm starting from behind. I need to do everything. I need to get everything on this campus working for me," which I think is not a way that most kids think when they arrive on campus, and definitely not kids from the South Side of Chicago.
<v Paul Tough>而且我认为对她而言，特别是在大学里产生的巨大影响的另一件事是这种足智多谋，是她拥有的那种感觉，即“我起步落后了。我需要做一切。我需要让这个校园里的一切为我所用”，我认为这不是大多数孩子在刚到校园时会想到的方式，更别提芝加哥南区的孩子了。

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:36.660
<v Kewauna Lerma>The first thing I did when I first got to every class, I introduced myself. First-- no, first, I sat in the front. If everybody else was sitting in the back, no. I sat in the front, like right on it. Like I'm nearly touching your desk and let them know, let them see my face, and let them remember it. And then after I go, the first step is to make sure they see your face, like they know who you are. As long as they know who you are.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我第一件事就是在每堂课开始时，做自我介绍。首先——不，首先，我坐在前面。如果其他人都坐在后面，不。我坐在前面，直接在那儿。我几乎摸到了你的桌子，让老师注意，让他们看到我的脸，让他们记住它。然后我去之后，第一步是确保他们看到你的脸，让他们知道你是谁。只要他们知道你是谁就行。

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:51.050
<v Kewauna Lerma>And I always, like after class or before class, I introduce myself. Ask them about their office hours. And have like a little calendar on my wall and say, "This day." I always did a check-in to see how I was doing.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我总是，在课后或课前，做自我介绍。询问他们的办公时间。墙上贴个小日历说，“这一天。”我总是做个签到，看看我做得如何。

00:49:51.050 --> 00:50:01.950
<v Kewauna Lerma>I will go to a teacher. I will go to a professor and get help. I have no shame in my game no more.
<v Kewauna Lerma>我会去找老师。我会去找教授寻求帮助。我再也不以寻求帮助为耻了。

00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:12.920
<v Ira Glass>Kewauna's instructor from One Goal stayed in touch with her and the other students from the class all through their first year of college. This was part of the plan, because it's so common for kids to drop out of college, especially for kids from poor neighborhoods.
<v Ira Glass>“一个目标”的指导员与凯瓦纳和其他学生在整个大一期间保持了联系。这是计划的一部分，因为孩子们辍学太常见了，特别是来自贫困社区的孩子。

00:50:12.920 --> 00:50:23.830
<v Ira Glass>This is one the surprising things that Paul Tough says that he learned as he wrote this book. The United States used to have a higher percentage of its young people graduate from college than any other country. Now, we're not even close.
<v Ira Glass>这是保罗·塔夫在写这本书时学到的令人惊讶的事情之一。美国曾经有更高比例的年轻人从大学毕业，超过任何其他国家。现在，我们甚至都比不上了。

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:32.520
<v Paul Tough>We're still doing pretty decently in terms of the number-- the percentage of kids, the percentage of say 18-year-olds, who go to college. The problem is--
<v Paul Tough>我们仍然在人数方面表现相当体面——孩子的比例，即18岁上大学的比例。问题是——

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:33.240
<v Ira Glass>Who enroll.
<v Ira Glass>入学的人。

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:41.200
<v Paul Tough>Who enroll, right. Who just show up there on the first day of school. But we also have the highest dropout rate in the world.
<v Paul Tough>入学的人，对吧。那些第一天上学就出现在那里的人。但我们也有世界上最高的辍学率。

00:50:41.200 --> 00:50:49.490
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to pause because that seems worth repeating. The United States has the highest college dropout rate in the industrialized world.
<v Ira Glass>我要暂停一下，因为这值得重复。美国拥有工业化国家中最高的大学辍学率。

00:50:49.490 --> 00:51:02.080
<v Paul Tough>It's an issue that we haven't, even in education policy circles, we haven't paid any attention to before very recently. Nobody was really focusing on it. We're very focused on getting kids to college.
<v Paul Tough>这是一个我们之前，甚至在教育政策圈内，直到最近才注意到，之前根本没注意的问题。没有人真正关注它。我们非常专注于送孩子上大学。

00:51:02.080 --> 00:51:18.700
<v Ira Glass>Of 128 students who signed up for One Goal at the same time as Kewauna, 85% of them have made it through their freshman year and are now back in school this fall as sophomores. Of course, they're still not done. Nationally, only about 58% of kids who start college end up graduating in six years or fewer.
<v Ira Glass>在与凯瓦纳同时报名参加“一个目标”的128名学生中，85%的人完成了大一学业，今年秋天作为大二学生回到了学校。当然，他们还没有毕业。在全国范围内，只有大约58%开始上大学的孩子能在六年或更短时间内毕业。

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:38.340
<v Ira Glass>But remember, Kewauna and these other kids in One Goal are average Chicago public high school students. They're not a select group. So the 85% is especially impressive. A University of Chicago study a few years ago showed that only 8% of high school freshman in Chicago will get a four year degree from college-- 8%.
<v Ira Glass>但请记住，凯瓦纳和“一个目标”里的这些孩子是普通的芝加哥公立高中学生。他们不是精选出来的。所以85%特别令人印象深刻。芝加哥大学几年前的一项研究显示，芝加哥只有8%的高中一年级学生能获得大学四年制学位——8%。

00:51:38.340 --> 00:51:49.450
<v Ira Glass>And of course, this is the hope that Heckman and other educators and researchers have for these non-cognitive skills, that programs like One Goal can teach them to kids like Kewauna, making those kids capable of seizing a better future for themselves.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这就是赫克曼和其他教育工作者及研究人员对这些非认知技能寄予的希望，即“一个目标”这样的项目可以教给像凯瓦纳这样的孩子，使这些孩子有能力为自己抓住一个更好的未来。

00:51:49.450 --> 00:51:59.700
<v James Heckman>And this, I think, just changes the way we think about poverty and human opportunity and what can we do to open up more possibilities to more people.
<v James Heckman>我认为这改变了我们对贫困、人类机会以及我们能做些什么来为更多人开启更多可能性的思考方式。

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:07.100
<v Ira Glass>When James Heckman talks about this stuff-- you know, economists can be such a sober, cautious bunch-- he can sound positively Utopian.
<v Ira Glass>当詹姆斯·赫克曼谈论这些时——你知道，经济学家可以是非常清醒、谨慎的一群人——他听起来绝对是乌托邦式的。

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:27.270
<v James Heckman>I think there's just a general question, which this whole research is about, which is, what are the determinants of human success? How fixed are these determinants? How much can you change them? How much can you bolster them? How much social policy can actually influence those? To me, that's extraordinarily interesting.
<v James Heckman>我认为有一个一般性的问题，也是这项研究的核心，那就是，人类成功的决定因素是什么？这些决定因素有多固定？你能改变多少？你能加强多少？社会政策实际上能在多大程度上影响这些？对我来说，这非常有趣。

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:42.300
<v Ira Glass>It's so interesting when you talk about this, because I feel like there's this vision of human perfectibility. Like, oh, our goal is we're going to make people so they'll be more successful and happy. Most of us don't even think about that as a possibility, because that goal is so big.
<v Ira Glass>当你谈论这个时非常有趣，因为我觉得这有一种人类完美主义的愿景。就像，哦，我们的目标是我们会让人们变得更成功、更快乐。我们大多数人甚至没想过这是一种可能性，因为那个目标太大了。

00:52:42.300 --> 00:52:50.410
<v James Heckman>Well, but I think one of the reasons why people don't think it's a possibility is they think these traits are fixed at birth or fixed so early there's not much we can do about it.
<v James Heckman>嗯，但我认为人们不认为这是可能的原因之一是他们认为这些特质在出生时就固定了，或者固定得太早，我们做不了什么。

00:52:50.410 --> 00:52:50.720
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:52:50.720 --> 00:53:10.250
<v James Heckman>And I think what we've learned is that these human capabilities can be shaped. And as an economist, what I like about it is that it has this possibility of reducing inequality, but not doing it through the standard mechanism of just handing out money and transfers from the rich to the poor. That's ancient.
<v James Heckman>而且我认为我们学到的是，这些人类能力是可以被塑造的。而且作为经济学家，我喜欢它的一点是，它有可能减少不平等，但不是通过仅仅从富人向穷人分发金钱和转移支付的标准机制。那太古老了。

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:20.380
<v James Heckman>The idea is you make the poor highly capable. That there really is a possibility of giving people more possibilities. That there really is the chance of improving their capabilities.
<v James Heckman>这个想法是让你使穷人变得非常有能力。确实存在给人们更多可能性的可能性。确实存在提高他们能力的机会。

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:45.760
<v James Heckman>And I'm personally very excited by that. And a lot of the evidence comes together, whether it's neuroscience, psychology, economics. It's the confluence of these things. There are these happy times in science and social science and knowledge where different strands come together. And I think we're at such a time.
<v James Heckman>而且我个人对此感到非常兴奋。而且很多证据汇聚在一起，无论是神经科学、心理学还是经济学。这些是科学、社会科学和知识中的快乐时光，不同的线索汇聚在一起。而且我认为我们正处于这样的时刻。

00:53:45.760 --> 00:54:05.580
<v Ira Glass>James Heckman. Paul Tough's book about all this is called How Children Succeed : Grit, Curiosity , and the Hidden Power of Character .
<v Ira Glass>詹姆斯·赫克曼。保罗·塔夫关于这一切的书叫《孩子们如何成功：坚毅、好奇心与品格的隐藏力量》（How Children Succeed: Grit, Curiosity, and the Hidden Power of Character）。

